WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.750
Film Courage: What’s the process of what
you do that we don’t see?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.879
Dale Stelly, Filmmaker: Good question.

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:15.559
With me being a producer, before I can direct
I need to go out there because my film either

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:18.700
lock in an investor or lock in a budget.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.070
A lot of times as me the filmmaker I’m spending
80 percent of the time finding an executive

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.810
producer or locking in funds and 20 percent
of the time creating a film.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.130
I want to switch it around.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.750
I want to spend 80 percent of the time shooting
a movie and only 20 percent of the time locking

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.150
in the investors.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:39.570
So what people don’t see is before I get
to the set they don’t know what I had to

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:45.040
go through to lock in a producer, lock in
a budget, be able to hire everybody, be able

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.970
to feed everybody, be able to have a situation
to shoot this movie.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.710
No one sees the business behind-the-scenes
side of it.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:54.710
They just see “Oh?

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.780
We’re shooting on the red cameras?

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.780
Oh?

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.930
We’re working with Vida Guerra or Malik
Whitfield today.”

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:00.930
You know what I mean?

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.950
They just see physical glitz and glamor part.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:12.030
They don’t really see like me in my home
office on the phone in email conjuring up

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:18.539
potential deals or potential relationships
that leads to producing movies or what have

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:21.789
you so people don’t see that part.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:22.869
People just think like “Oh?

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:25.219
The camera just shows up.”

00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:31.521
And the craft service just pays for itself…well
I don’t think they think that but people

00:01:31.521 --> 00:01:41.090
just don’t see what it takes to get a production
like financing and going and meeting deadlines.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.659
It looks easy and it looks like we’re having
fun on set when we are working with certain

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:53.609
talent and certain equipment and stuff but
it’s not easy it just looks easy.

00:01:53.609 --> 00:01:59.880
Film Courage: Going back to that one set in
1995 when you were doing craft service, did

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.079
you think it was easy from watching your friend
[referring to a story Dale us in a prior interview]?

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:06.770
Dale: You know what, it did look easy.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:19.300
I remember being 19 or 20…yes I was 20 in
’95 and I remember my boy Daniel Zirilli

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:27.230
sitting in the chair (the director chair)
and all this dude was saying was “Action!

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.010
Action!

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.790
Action!”

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.549
I’m like “Yo?

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:35.760
Wait a minute?”

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.150
That’s all he is doing is this directing
the talent and the talent and the talent is

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.959
doing everything.

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:52.549
He made me figure out how to get in that chair
and it was not easy at all.

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:56.090
It looks easy once you are on the set and
you’re like “Hey roll camera, roll sound.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.099
We’ve got speed…okay action!”

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:05.099
All that stuff looks fun and easy but no one
knows that you first of all had to have a

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:09.500
body of work to even have booked the job that
was going on.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:15.170
You need to have a reel, you need to have
a website, you needed to say the right thing

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:22.220
and answer the questions professionally and
speak articulately.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:28.849
Everything matters before you get to set and
some people don’t realize that it takes

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:34.010
a lot of hard work to get in that director’s
chair to be to be working with big names or

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.469
big companies.

00:03:36.469 --> 00:03:40.340
It takes a lot of success to get to that point.

